<h2 class="bde-heading-390-178 bde-heading">
Where Do the Mavericks Sit Against the Salary Cap?
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-211 bde-shortcode"><p>Dallas Mavericks salaries add up to a cap payroll of <strong>$189.07 million</strong> for 2026–27, <strong>$24.11 million</strong> above the <strong>$164.96 million</strong> salary cap. That figure includes <strong>$11.87 million</strong> in dead cap on top of a standard roster of <strong>$177.19 million</strong>.</p>
<p>Kyrie Irving tops the payroll at <strong>$39.49 million</strong>, ahead of P.J. Washington on <strong>$19.81 million</strong> and Daniel Gafford on <strong>$17.26 million</strong>, together about <strong>43.2%</strong> of the roster total. Their tax salary is <strong>$11.10 million</strong> below the <strong>$200.43 million</strong> luxury-tax line and apron salary sits <strong>$18.34 million</strong> under the <strong>$209.02 million</strong> first apron.</p>
</div><div class="bde-shortcode-390-179 bde-shortcode"><div class="nst nst-component-cap" style="--nst-accent:#00538C"><p>The Dallas Mavericks standard roster accounts for $177.19M. Add $11.87M in dead cap to reach $189.07M before cap holds. Holds are separate accounting charges, not signed salary payments.</p><div class="nst-threshold-grid"><div><h3>Salary Cap</h3><strong>$164.96M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:100%"></i></div><p>$24.11M above<br><small>Using cap payroll before holds</small></p></div><div><h3>Luxury-Tax Line</h3><strong>$200.43M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:94.46304907498%"></i></div><p>$11.10M below<br><small>Using tax salary</small></p></div><div><h3>First Apron</h3><strong>$209.02M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:91.225502954333%"></i></div><p>$18.34M below<br><small>Using apron salary</small></p></div><div><h3>Second Apron</h3><strong>$221.69M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:86.011288489124%"></i></div><p>$31.01M below<br><small>Using apron salary</small></p></div></div><div class="nst-tax-panel"><h3>Estimated Luxury-Tax Bill</h3><div class="nst-tax-summary"><strong>$0.00M</strong><span>Standard tax rates</span></div><p>The recorded tax salary is below the tax line, so this snapshot produces no luxury-tax bill. This is an estimate, not the final season-end assessment.</p><div class="nst-tax-history"><span><b>2022–23</b>Taxpayer</span><span><b>2023–24</b>Below tax</span><span><b>2024–25</b>Taxpayer</span><span><b>2025–26</b>Below tax</span></div><p class="nst-note">Three or more taxpayer seasons in the previous four trigger repeater rates. Status reviewed 2026-09-16.</p></div><h3>What Does Going Over the Tax Line Mean?</h3><p>The luxury tax is an additional team expense. It rises in bands as tax salary moves above the threshold; teams with a recent history of paying tax can face higher repeater rates. The final bill depends on the season-end calculation.</p><h3>Why the Aprons Matter</h3><p>Aprons affect how a team can build its roster, including certain exceptions and trades. Being below an apron does not automatically create cap space, and a payroll figure alone does not establish whether a hard cap has been triggered.</p><p class="nst-note">Cap holds: $24.12M. Threshold comparisons describe the recorded salary totals; they do not establish available signing room.</p></div></div>
</section><section id="team-eras" class="bde-fancy-container-390-172 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<h2 class="bde-heading-390-182 bde-heading">
Dallas Mavericks’ History and Payroll Eras
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-183 bde-shortcode"><div class="nst nst-component-eras" style="--nst-accent:#00538C"><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>1980 · Dallas Joins the NBA</summary><p>The Mavericks were established as an expansion franchise in 1980.</p></details><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>2011 · Nowitzki’s Championship</summary><p>Dirk Nowitzki led Dallas to its first NBA championship in 2011.</p></details><h3>The Payroll Over Time</h3><p>Across the four completed seasons shown, cap payroll ranged from $163.63M to $182.78M. The current roster puts 43.21% of active cap payroll into its three largest contracts.</p></div></div>
</section><section id="team-contracts" class="bde-fancy-container-390-173 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<h2 class="bde-heading-390-184 bde-heading">
Mavericks Contracts and Future Salaries
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-212 bde-shortcode"><p>Mavericks contracts and future salaries run further than most. Washington’s veteran extension is worth <strong>$88.76 million</strong> and lasts to <strong>2029–30</strong>, and Naji Marshall’s <strong>$51.86 million</strong> extension, agreed in August, starts next season and runs to the same year. Gafford is signed through 2028–29 at <strong>$54.38 million</strong>.</p>
<p>Irving’s <strong>$118.47 million</strong> agreement ends in 2027–28. Two deals expire after this season, the rookie contracts of Dereck Lively II and Marcus Sasser.</p>
